A running track is a ring formed by two concentric circles. So, imagine a circular track, like a running race track at a stadium. The inner edge and the outer edge of the track both form circles, right? And these two circles are concentric, meaning they have the same center. So the track itself is the region between the two circles. The width of the track would then be the distance between the two circles, essentially the radial distance from the inner circle to the outer circle. That is, if you draw a radius from the common center to the outer circle, and then from the same center to the inner circle, the difference between those two radii lengths is the width of the track.

Now, the problem states that the circumferences of the two circles differ by 10π feet. So the outer circumference minus the inner circumference is 10π feet. Since circumference of a circle is given by the formula C = 2πr, where r is the radius, so if I let the radius of the inner circle be r, then the radius of the outer circle must be r plus the width of the track, right? Let me define some variables here to make it clearer.

